Tests for the patches in patches/glib2.0/, run by hand in a container and in GitLab CI, see .gitlab-ci.yml.

Scope before exec: run-scope-test.sh, scope_test.py, fake_systemd.py and fake_journald.py
    Checks scope-before-exec.patch against a systemd user instance, which the tests GLib's build runs cannot have.
    run-scope-test.sh starts one as a plain process, the way user@.service starts it, in a cgroup delegated to an
    unprivileged user, and runs scope_test.py in each scenario as that user. scope_test.py launches a test
    application through GDesktopAppInfo, with the GLib under test, which starts two processes the moment it runs,
    as Chromium starts its zygotes, and then checks which cgroup each of the three processes is in:

      loop              launched from a service of the user instance, as GNOME Shell's, which keeps running: every
                        process is in app-gnome-glib\x2dscope\x2dtest-<pid>.scope, which the "launched" signal names
      pid-namespace     the same, from a PID namespace of its own, which still uses the session bus and the cgroups
                        of the user instance, as in a sandbox like Firejail's: the same, as GLib gives systemd a pidfd
      exit              the same as loop, but the launching process exits right after launching: the same
      late-request      the launching process asks for the same scope after GLib, as gnome_start_systemd_scope()
                        does: its request fails with UnitExists, and every process is in the scope
      no-systemd        launched on a session bus of its own, without systemd: no scope is asked for, and the
                        application starts at once, in the launching process's cgroup
      no-systemd-exit   the same, but the launching process exits right after launching: the same
      refused           launched on a session bus of its own, on which fake_systemd.py owns systemd's name and refuses
                        every scope: the scope is asked for, refused, and the application starts at once, in the
                        launching process's cgroup, and gio-launch-desktop logs the refusal at error priority
      refused-exit      the same, but the launching process exits right after launching, so that nothing tells
                        gio-launch-desktop about the refusal: the application starts after the 5 seconds
                        gio-launch-desktop waits at most, and it logs at error priority that the scope did not exist
      outside           launched outside of the user instance: no scope is asked for, and the application starts at
                        once

    In every scenario the application also has to start in time and see none of the variables through which GLib
    talks to gio-launch-desktop, and gio-launch-desktop has to log nothing but what is described above. The
    container has no journald, so run-scope-test.sh starts fake_journald.py, which listens on /dev/log and
    /run/systemd/journal/stdout, and the launching process connects its standard error, which gio-launch-desktop
    inherits, to the latter, as GNOME Shell does for the applications it launches. gio-launch-desktop then logs
    through syslog, so that its messages keep their priority, which the journal digest selects by.

    run-scope-test.sh has to run as root in a privileged container with a cgroup namespace of its own, so that it
    can create cgroups for the user instance, and refuses to run elsewhere. The container needs systemd, dbus-daemon
    and dbus-user-session for the user instance and its session bus, and python3-gi. The GLib to test is used from a
    directory its libglib2.0-0t64 package was extracted into, with LD_LIBRARY_PATH and GIO_LAUNCH_DESKTOP, so the
    installed GLib stays as it is. With rootless Docker, from the root of the repository:

      mkdir /tmp/glib-root
      dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/libglib2.0-0t64_*.deb /tmp/glib-root
      docker run --rm --privileged -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" -v /tmp/glib-root:/glib-root:ro \
        registry.gitlab.com/tozd/docker/base:ubuntu-resolute bash -c '
          apt-get update -q && apt-get install -y -q --no-install-recommends dbus dbus-user-session python3 \
            python3-gi gir1.2-glib-2.0 systemd util-linux && useradd --create-home tester &&
          /repo/patches/glib2.0/tests/run-scope-test.sh /glib-root /tmp/scope-test tester'

    Each check prints a line starting with PASS or FAIL, and the script exits with 0 only when all passed. Without the
    patch, every scenario but no-systemd, no-systemd-exit and outside fails: no scope is named, and every process
    stays in the cgroup of the service which launched it. OUT_DIR receives the log of the user instance, what
    fake_journald.py received in journal/, and for each scenario the test application's desktop file, what the
    application and the launching process reported, and result.json.
